{
 "id": "2022-li-community",
 "name": "Community Space Weather - Thermal Perception and Landscape Quality",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original images: 20 SketchUp renderings of the same public space in an old residential community, viewed from 160 cm with a 28 mm lens, built from the features most common in 921 photographs of such spaces and varied along five dimensions with four levels each: natural enrichment (A1-A4), sky visibility (B1-B4), shading measures (C1-C4), floor covering material (D1-D4) and activity type (E1-E4). Participants answer background questions on the variables the paper analysed (gender, age group, southern or northern city, years of residence, how often they use the community's public space, how long they stay, education, occupation and monthly income), are assigned at random to the paper's comfortable-weather or hot-weather group and read that group's scenario text, and then see one image chosen at random, as the paper showed each participant only one image. They rate it on the paper's six 7-point bipolar items (Table 2): thermal perceptual schema vote (It would be very cold to be in this scene - It would be very hot to be in this scene), four landscape quality items (not beautiful at all - very beautiful, very boring - very interesting, not attractive to me at all - very attractive to me, doesn't appeal to me at all - very appealing to me) and activity willingness (I do not want to do activities in this scene - I want to do activities in this scene). Rebuilt from Li, Wu, Xu, Zhong & Wang (2022), How Thermal Perceptual Schema Mediates Landscape Quality Evaluation and Activity Willingness, Int. J. Environ. Res. Public Health 19(20): 13681, with the images cropped from its Figure 1, the scenario texts from Section 3.3 and the items, printed verbatim, from Table 2 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: A1-E4 = dimension A-E and level 1-4 of Figure 1; answers are in ratings, keyed by image id, with item codes TPSV, LQE1-LQE4 and ACT, and the weather group is in weather_group; each image's dimension and level are stored in the page's original-study block. Analysis as in the paper: mean of the four LQE items as the landscape quality score; within each weather group, one-way ANOVA with LSD post hoc tests of TPSV, LQE and activity willingness across the four levels of each dimension; binary logistic regression of hot versus cold TPSV on the background variables, with socioeconomic status from education, occupation and income; and mediation of activity willingness by TPS and LQE. The paper (3435 volunteers recruited online, living in southern and northern cities: 1773 comfortable weather, 1662 hot weather) found that more greenery lowered the perceived heat and raised landscape quality in both groups, more open sky raised the perceived heat, socioeconomic status and age were the main sources of bias in thermal perception, and landscape quality mattered more for activity willingness in pleasant weather while thermal perception mattered more in hot weather. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper's method text speaks of 15 scenarios, but its Figure 1 and results tables use 20 images (A1-E4), so all 20 are used here; (2) the wording and answer options of the background questions are ours, following the groups in the paper's Table 3, because the paper does not print the questions; the filler questions the paper added to disguise its aim are not included; (3) the paper gave each volunteer a reward; here anyone can take part; (4) the images are the figure copies (about 545 px wide).",
